Date: Mon, 28 Dec 92 20:06:21 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Let's Admit We May Disagree

Since this is a list where we are supposed to be able to take some
basics for granted, let's try to get past rehearsing the speeches we'd
give others about "PPL", and work more to unpack this concept.  It
seems to me that the phrase PPL (and others that are taken as synonyms
here) is used very broadly here, actually including a wide range of
rather different concepts.  Since we're among friends here, let's risk
seeing where we disagree.

Here are some variations I've heard (I've made up names for some):

VOLENTARY LAW:  Law where you signed the social contract enforcing it.

CUSTOMARY LAW:  Law intended mainly to explicate customs, made largely
  by judges through precident. 

INSURED LAW: Your insurer pays fines you owe, and guarentees
  compensation per crime against you, regardless of law of offender.

Not only are these all different, but I'll now declare that none of
these is what I mean by "PPL".  Well, actually, I'll defer to common
or first usage of the term; what I mean is none of these is what I
really want.

Volentary law, per se, isn't what I want.  If aliens appeared above
and threatened to destroy any government that claimed juristiction
over someone whe hadn't signed up with them, governments could just
insist that all previous citizens sign up or be deported.  Deportation
needn't be considered anti-volentary if current states can be said to
have already established property rights to control who lives within
their territory.  Unless you quibble about who "really" owns the land
people now live on, this way all law becomes volentary, but not more
to my liking.

Customary law isn't what I want, at least not now.  As Hal describes,
I wouldn't mind if the laws I could choose from were dreamed up by
clever law marketeers, rather that judges feeling themselves
constrained to explicating custom.

And insured law isn't what I want.  If I try to buy insurance so that
I get paid a certain amount if someone waives a gun at me (as in
Rebecca's example), regardless of the law they have, that will cost me
a lot more than a law where compensation depends on my offender's law.
I want it to be in my interest to adjust my behavior to the law of
folks I interact with.  And moral hazard considerations (that being
insured might make me less careful) may often make it cheaper for me
to self-insure about the fines I may have to pay others.

Here are some things I do want:

CIVIL LAW: It's not a crime unless a victim complains, and fines paid
  just go (as much as possible) to the victim and to pay expenses of
  enforcing that crime.  

UNBUNDLED LAW: Encourage competition by enabling seperate contracts in
  where you live, where you work, who you socialize with, what laws
  you live under, who enforces them, who arbitrates them, who changes 
  them, who insurers you, who protects you etc.

These are both just tendencies and directions I want to go, without
sharp boundaries of when you are "there".  So I'm uncomfortable with
either/or questions like Harry asked, about whether civil disobedience
makes sense under a PPL.  Under the above concepts, it would hopefully
become less needed, but would still be possible and meaningful.

