Date: Mon, 28 Dec 1992 23:14-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: Re: PPL: Let's Admit We May Disagree

Robin's and Rebecca's comments got me thinking again about what kind of
PPL setup I'd like.  I'm going to sketch out the kind of legal framework 
I would try to arrange.  Along the way, I'll look for contracts which
have the following properties if possible:

  1. Unbundled.         [ I want to be able to be selective & shop around. ]
  2. Easy exit.         [ I want to be able to change my mind cheaply. ]
  3. Non-exclusive.     [ I want to shop around even while under contract. ]
  4. Arbitrated.        [ What happens when we disagree? ]

Please do suggest other items to add to this list.  As I suggested a few
months ago, I think it would make a great Extropy article to outline some 
pointers on "How to be an alert anarcho-capitalist."  It's not going to be
easy to arrive at the right PPL's -- if we're not alert, we'll end up back
where we started.

---

Regarding details of the kind of infrastructure I'd like:  I would
personally sign up for the following if they were available.

   Living arrangements:  a neighborhood-sized PPL environment; the
   operator insures me against theft and violence within the neighborhood, 
   enforces a fairly lax set of rules about noise, littering, etc., and
   ideally is a member of a standardized legal framework which extends to
   all neighborhoods that I regularly visit.  The operator's physical
   control of the neighborhood reduces costs.

   Laws:  I'd choose a very forgiving set of laws, and seek out a region
   where most people around me choose similarly.

   Restaurants & shopping:  I'd be a member of one or several Consumer
   Reports style organizations, and use their recommendations when in
   doubt about honesty or health concerns.  Hopefully most establishments
   would display the symbols of one or more well-known consumer protection
   agencies.  [ Problem: I don't have any trademark laws, so how can the
   agencies prevent misuse of their symbols?  Instead of trying to enforce 
   laws against trademark infringement, perhaps I should adopt a technological
   solution, and electronically query the consumer agency rather than relying 
   on sanctioned use of the agency's symbol. ]

   Physical protection outside my neighborhood:  I'd look for an umbrella
   legal protection agency that guarantees to defend me against anybody
   and everybody.  This sounds expensive, but my premiums should be
   reduced because I live in a safe (and insured) neighborhood, and by my
   agreeing to pay a fairly sizeable deductible/co-insurance.

   Contract enforcement: I'd choose an agency and name it on my contracts,
   in addition to an arbitration agency.

   Health insurance:  much like today.  I'd choose a high deductible and
   a fairly restrictive contract, to reduce costs.

   Unemployment insurance, welfare for me:  none.  I'd rely on my family.
   If I had dependents, I'd get life/disability insurance.

   Roads: I'd look to become a member of a driving club which can
   provide cost and safety information and perhaps negotiate for me.

   National defense & weapons control:  I don't want to own guns or rocket
   launchers, nor do I particularly want to live next to someone who does.
   I'm leaning towards moving somewhere with a limited regional government
   to provide protection and weapons control (which seem to require
   control of physical territory).  I plan to protect myself with
   information and computer networks, not guns.  [ Problem: the
   authorities will have more firepower than I will.  How about this
   solution: divide the enforcement responsibility among N cooperating
   agencies, and each person living in the region must pay an amount K per
   year to some subset of the agencies or be forcibly deported by all of them.
   I think this is better than democracy, but I'm not totally sure.  At
   least I can personally choose how to allocate my own resources, even if
   there's a floor on how much I must contribute. ]


A key to my confidence in the system will be the availability of extensive 
consumer-reports information, and I'll be willing to spend a lot for it.
Good information can substitute for laws in a lot of cases, and I'm
happier with that kind of solution.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

