> 1. "How would a libertarian (free-market, Capitalist, etc.) handle the 
> following problem: .......... (environment, homeless, etc.)?"
> This is  a l w a y s  an attempt to change the subject from valid criticism
> of government agression against innocent citizens to the supposed flaws
> of Capitalism. 

No, its pointing out that there are problems with other economic systems,
and asking how your system would solve them better. Saying that these problems
are just a result of government agression may make you feel ideologically
justified in disliking the government, but it convinces no one. It is a flaw
in capitalism, communism, and most other economic systems so far, why will
your system solve the problem, a nice concrete proposal please.
 
> why would their noble, selfless concerns suddenly go away in a free society?

You can be noble and selfless without expecting others to be so. There are 
other good reasons to worry about these problems, as well (like people who might
do something not being informed). I am not even particularly concerned that 
the people on this list would not activelyt help the homeless in some way. I am
concerned about the millions of people who watch crap TV and regularly vote in
morons. They are quite capable of acting against their self-interest, let alone
societal good. Why should living in libertarian paradise make them smarter?
	This is not a condemnation of libertarianism, other systems face the
problem just as much. But how is libertarianism going to overcome the problem? 

> The implicit implication is that there would bo more problems in a free
> society (more homeless, more environmental damage, etc.). This is, of course,
> absolute nonsense, even opponents of Capitalism admit that it is more
> efficient in supplying anything, including housing and fresh air. Go breathe
> the air in Minsk, and then in New York. Go look at Volga and then at the
> Ohio River.
And West Germany, and Sweden. Comparisons like this are spurious. I can prove
anything with isolated examples. There are so many other variables than just
economy that your examples prove very little. Opponents of capitalism admit
that it is more sucessful on its own terms, but might not agree with its 
assumptions. There is significant reason to believe that capitalism is not 
particularly efficient at delivering clean air. When the air is clean, and it
is manifestly a result of a capitalist air cleaning system, then it is no
longer in significant doubt. 

> 2. How would the government be able to perform any economic function when
> it already proved over and over that it cannot perform even the simplest
> economic calculation? The government uses force, thus it can only justify
> its use if it substitutes for citizens' self-defence. It can't perform any
> economic functions because it does not face economic risks, and thus can't
> have the ability to perform economic calculations.
Hmm... I know what you are trying to say, but this paragraph reads rather 
confusedly. After all, the government does face some economic risks, at least
second-hand (spectacularly poor performance causes it too lose office), and
saying that you need to take the economic risk in order to make the calculation
seems to be saying that the entire subject of economics is pretty irrelevent.

The big thing to remember is that criticisms of libertarianism that say 'how
are you going to solve this problem?' are valid. If you want it to become
a political system that is actually used, then say how it would answer these
problems in some concrete form. I'm not saying that it can't be done, but until
you show me, your politics is at best too vague. Take criticism as a useful
pointer for possible problems, not as an attack. Then everyojne might learn 
something.

							Dave Cake
PS I understand that many people on this list may well have much more specific
ideas about how to solve these problems, and no offence is intended. I am 
commneting on a single post, not on libertarianism as a whole.

